GraphQL Development Rules

Schema Design and Definition
==========================

- Design clear, concise, and semantic GraphQL schemas using SDL
- Use PascalCase for type names (e.g., User, Product)
- Use camelCase for field names and arguments (e.g., firstName, productId)
- Provide clear descriptions for types, fields, and arguments
- Establish explicit relationships between types
- Favor non-nullable fields where appropriate
- Utilize enums for fixed sets of possible values
- Implement interfaces for common field sets
- Use unions for fields with multiple possible types
- Version schemas carefully

Query and Mutation Design
=======================

- Design queries specific to client data requirements
- Implement efficient filtering, sorting, and pagination
- Structure mutations to clearly indicate input and changes
- Follow consistent naming conventions for mutations
- Design mutations to return sufficient outcome information

Resolver Implementation
=====================

- Write efficient and well-organized resolvers
- Separate data fetching logic from core resolver logic
- Implement proper error handling within resolvers
- Utilize asynchronous programming for data fetching
- Implement data loaders to solve N+1 problem
- Ensure resolvers respect authorization rules
- Optimize database queries and API calls
- Cache frequently accessed data

Security
=======

- Implement robust authentication and authorization
- Validate all user-provided input
- Be mindful of security vulnerabilities
- Implement rate limiting
- Sanitize user-provided data
- Protect against denial-of-service attacks

Performance Optimization
=====================

- Analyze query performance using appropriate tools
- Optimize database queries based on access patterns
- Implement caching at various levels
- Utilize persisted queries
- Consider query batching
- Monitor performance metrics

